And if your hand or your foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away from you. It is better for you to enter life crippled or lame than to be thrown into the eternal fire with both your hands and both your feet.
And if your eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and throw it away from you. It is better for you to enter life with one eye than to be thrown into hell with two eyes. Like a lost sheep.
Look, do not despise one of these little ones, for I tell you that their angels in heaven always behold the face of My Father who is in heaven.
For the Son of Man has come to save what was lost.
What do you think? If a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray, does he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go after the lost one?
And if it happens that he finds it, truly I say to you, he rejoices over it more than over the ninety-nine that did not stray.
Thus is not the will of your Father who is in heaven that one of these little ones should perish. If your brother sins against you,
And if your brother sins against you, go and rebuke him in private between you and him alone. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
And if he will not listen, take with you one or two more, so that every matter may be established by the mouth of two or three witnesses.
And if he does not listen to them, tell it to the church. And if he does not listen to the church, let him be to you as a Gentile and a tax collector.
Truly I tell you, whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And I tell you also, that if two of you agree on earth about anything you ask for, it will be done for you by my Father who is in heaven.
"For where two or three gather in my name, there am I among them.” Like a servant who does not forgive."
Then Peter came to Him and said, “Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? Up to seven times?”
And Jesus said to him, “I do not say to you seven times, but seventy times seven.”
Therefore the kingdom of heaven is like a man who was a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
And when he began the accounting, someone indebted to him with ten thousand weights came forward.
And as he had nothing to pay off his master’s command, he was to be sold, along with his wife and children and all that he possessed, to settle the debt.
The slave boasted and prostrated himself to him, saying: "O master, be patient with me, and I will repay you all."
He freed his slave and released him, leaving him to his religion.
And when that slave went out, he found one of his fellow slaves, who owed him a hundred dinars, so he seized him and took him by the throat, saying, “Pay me what you owe me.”
The freedman’s pride is his companion at his feet, and he asked him, saying: “Be patient with me, and I will repay you all.”
But he did not return, rather he went on and cast him into prison until the debt is paid.
And when the slaves saw what had befallen their companions, they were deeply saddened. They came and recounted to their master all that had happened.
Then his master called to him and said to him: “O wicked servant, all that debt I forgave you because you asked me.”
Should you not have had mercy on your fellow servant as I had mercy on you?
And his master was angry and delivered him to the tormentors until he paid all that was due to him.
Thus does your Heavenly Father do to you if you do not each forgive your brother his trespasses.
Marriage and divorce
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went to the region of Judea, beyond the Jordan.
And large crowds followed Him, and He healed them there.
And the Pharisees came to Him to test Him, asking, “Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?”
And he answered and said to them, “Have you not read that He who created from the beginning made them male and female?”
And he said, “For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united with his wife, and the two will become one flesh.”
“They are not two, but one body. And what God has joined together, let no one separate.”
They said to him, “Why did Moses recommend giving a divorce document and then divorcing?”
He said to them, “Because of your hardness of heart Moses permitted you to divorce your wives. But from the beginning it was not so.”
And I say to you, whoever divorces his wife except for lewdness and marries another commits adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery.”
His disciples said to him, “If this is the case for a man and his wife, it is better not to marry!”
And He said to them, “Not everyone accepts this word, but those to whom it is given.”
For there are eunuchs who were born thus from their mothers’ wombs, and there are eunuchs who were made eunuchs by men, and there are eunuchs who have made themselves eunuchs for the kingdom of heaven’s sake. He who is able to receive this, let him receive it.” Jesus blessed the children.
Then people brought little children to Jesus for him to place his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples rebuked them.
But Jesus said, “Let the little children come to me and do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of heaven.”
So he laid his hands on them and departed from there. The young man, wealthy.
And one came up to Him and said, “Teacher, what good thing shall I do to inherit eternal life?”
And he said to him, “Why do you call me good? No one is good except God alone. But if you want to enter life, keep the commandments.”
He said to Him, “Which of the commandments?” And Jesus said, “You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness.”
Honor your father and your mother, and love your neighbor as yourself.
The young man said to him, “I have memorized all of this since my youth. What more do I need?”
And Jesus said to him, “If you want to be perfect, go and sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me.”
And when the young man heard the word, he went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions.
And Jesus said to his disciples, “Truly I tell you, it is hard for a rich man to enter the kingdom of heaven!”
And I say to you also, that it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God!».
And when His disciples heard it, they were greatly astonished, saying, “Who then can be saved?”
And Jesus looked at them and said, “With people this is impossible, but with God all things are possible.”
Then Peter answered and said to Him, “See, we have left all and followed You. What shall we have?”
And Jesus said to them, “Truly I say to you, that you who have followed me, in the regeneration when the Son of Man sits on his glorious throne, you also will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.”
And whoever leaves houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or fields for my name’s sake will receive a hundredfold and inherit eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
Like the doers in generosity.
For the kingdom of heaven is like a man who was the master of a house, who went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard,
So he agreed with the workers on a dinar per day, and sent them to his vineyard.
Then he went out around the third hour and saw others standing in the marketplace, idle.
And He said to them, “Go you also into the vineyard, and whatever is right I will give you.” So they went.
And he went out again about the sixth and ninth hour and did the same.
Then about eleven o'clock he went out and found others standing idle, and he said to them, “Why have you been standing here all day idle?”
They said to him, “Because no one hired us.” He said to them, “Go you also to the vineyard and take what is rightfully yours.”
And when evening came, the generous master said to his servant, “Call the workers and pay them their wages, beginning with the last and going to the first.”
Then came the companions of the eleventh hour and they took a denarius each.
And when the first ones came, they thought they were taking more. So they took a dinar each as well.
And while they were taking [what they needed], they complained to the master of the house.
Saying: “These others worked one hour, and they have been made equal to us who bore the burden of the day and the heat!”
So he answered and said to one of them, “Friend, I have done you no wrong! Did we not agree on a denarius?”
Take your leg and go, for I want to give to this last one like you.
Or do you begrudge me the right to do as I please with my own? Is your envy aroused because I am generous?
Thus the last will be first, and the first last, for many are called, but few are chosen.” Jesus foretells his death and resurrection.
Now as Jesus was going up to Jerusalem, He took the twelve disciples aside on the way and said to them:
And behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered to the chief priests and scribes, and they will condemn Him to death,
“and they will hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and flogged and crucified, and on the third day he will rise.” Zebedee’s son asked.
Then the mother of Zebedee’s sons came to Him with her sons, kneeling and asking a favor of Him.
He said to her, “What do you want?” She said to him, “Say that these two sons of mine may sit, one at your right and the other at your left, in your kingdom.”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “You do not know what you are asking. Are you able to drink the cup that I am about to drink, and to be baptized with the baptism with which I am baptized?” They said to Him, “We are able.”
He said to them, “As for my cup, you both drink from it, and you will be dyed with the same dye that I am dyed with.” And as for sitting on my right and on my left, I cannot grant it except to those for whom my father has prepared it.”
And when the ten heard this, they became indignant over the two brothers.
Then Jesus called them and said, “You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and the great ones exercise authority over them.
So it shall not be among you. But whoever would be great among you must be your servant,
And whoever wants to be first among you must be your slave.
“For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.” Healing of two blind men in Jericho.
And as they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd followed Him.
And two blind men were sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, “Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!”
Then the crowd rebuked them, telling them to be silent, but they cried out all the more, saying: “Have mercy on us, Lord, Son of David!”
So Jesus stood and called out to them, saying, “What do you want me to do for you?”
They said to him, “Lord, let our eyes be opened!”
And Jesus had compassion on them and touched their eyes, and immediately their eyes regained sight, and they followed him.
Entry into Jerusalem
And when they drew near to Jerusalem and came to Bethphage and the Mount of Olives, then Jesus sent two disciples.
saying to them, “Go to the village ahead of you, and as soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey tied and a colt with it. Untie them and bring them to me.”
And if anyone says anything to you, say: “The Lord has need of them.” And at once He will send them away.”
And all this was done that what was spoken by the prophet might be fulfilled:
Tell the daughter of Zion: Behold, your king is coming to you, humble, and mounted on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey.
So the two disciples went and did as Jesus had commanded them.
And they brought the donkey and the colt and put their garments on them, and He sat on them.
And the majority spread their garments in the road. And others cut branches from the trees and spread them in the road.
And the crowds who went before and who followed were shouting, “Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!”
And when He entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred, saying, “Who is this?”
And the crowd said, “This is Jesus the prophet from Nazareth of Galilee.” Cleansing of the temple.
And Jesus entered the temple of God and drove out all those who were selling and buying in the temple, and overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those who sold doves.
And He said to them, “It is written, ‘My house shall be called a house of prayer,’ but you have made it a den of thieves!”
And his uncle came to him and limped in the temple, and he healed them.
When the chief priests and the scribes saw the wonders that he did, and the children crying in the temple and saying, “Hosanna to the Son of David!” they were indignant.
And they said to Him, “Do You hear what these are saying?” And Jesus said to them, “Yes! Have you never read: ‘Out of the mouths of babes and infants You have perfected praise’?”
Then he left them and went outside the city to Bethany, where he spent the night. The fig tree withered.
And in the morning, as He returned to the city, He was hungry.
So he saw a fig tree by the road, and came to it and found nothing on it but leaves only. And he said to it, “May there never come fruit from you again!” And the fig tree withered at once.
And when the disciples saw it, they marveled, saying, “How did the fig tree wither so quickly?”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “Truly I say to you, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will do not only what I have shown you with the fig tree, but also if you say to this mountain, ‘Be removed and be cast into the sea,’ it will be so.”
“And whatever you ask in prayer, believing, you will receive.” Question about the authority of Jesus.
And when He came to the temple, the chief priests and elders of the people approached Him while He was teaching, saying, “By what authority are You doing these things? And who gave You this authority?”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “I also ask you one word: if you tell me about it, I will also tell you by what authority I do this.”
“John’s baptism: was it from heaven or from men?” They reasoned among themselves, saying, “If we say, ‘From heaven,’ He will say to us, ‘Why did you not believe him?’”
And if we say: “From the people, we fear the populace, for John is like a prophet to all.”
So they answered Jesus and said, “We do not know.” And He said to them also, “Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things. Like the two sons.”
What do you think? A man had two sons. He came to the first and said to him, “Son, go and work today in my vineyard.”
So he answered and said, “I do not want.” But he finally regretted it and went.
And he came to the second and said the same. So he answered and said, “Here I am, Lord.” And he did not pass by.
“Which of the two did the will of his father?” They said to him, “The first.” Jesus said to them, “Truly I say to you, the tax collectors and prostitutes go ahead of you into the kingdom of God.”
For John came to you in the way of righteousness and you did not believe him, but the tax collectors and prostitutes did believe him. And even after you saw this, you did not repent and believe him. Like the vineyard keepers.
Listen to another parable: A man planted a vineyard and set a hedge around it, and dug a winepress in it and built a tower, and leased it to tenants and went away.
And when the time of the fruit drew near, he sent his servants to the tenant farmers to get his fruit.
So the honorable men took his slaves and flogged some, killed some, and stoned some.
Then he sent others still more, and they were treated the same way.
So finally he sent his son to them, saying: “They fear my son!”
Now as for the honorable ones, when they saw the son, they said among themselves, “This is the heir! Come, let us kill him and take his inheritance!”
So they took him and dragged him outside the vineyard and killed him.
So when the Lord of generosity comes, what will He do with those generous ones?
They said to him, “Those evildoers will be destroyed by a wretched destruction, and nobility will be passed on to other noble people who will give its fruits in their seasons.”
Then Jesus said to them, “Have you never read in the Scriptures: ‘The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone—by the Lord this has been done, and it is marvelous in our eyes’?”
Therefore I say to you, the kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a nation producing its fruits.
And whoever falls on this stone will be shattered, and whoever it falls on will be crushed!
And when the chief priests and Pharisees heard his parables, they perceived that he spoke of them.
And as they sought to arrest Him, they feared the crowds, because they regarded Him as a prophet.
Like a royal wedding.
And Jesus began to speak to them in parables, saying:
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who made a wedding feast for his son,
And he sent his servants to call those who had been invited to the wedding, but they were unwilling to come.
So he sent other servants, saying, “Tell those who are invited, ‘See, my dinner is prepared. My oxen and fattened cattle have been slaughtered, and everything is ready. Come to the wedding!’”
But they were negligent and went on their way, one to his field, and another to his business.
And the rest seized their slaves, insulted them, and killed them.
When the king heard this, he was furious and sent his soldiers, destroying those murderers and burning their city.
Then he said to his servants, ‘The wedding is ready, but those who were invited are not worthy.’
So go to the crossroads, and invite everyone you find to the wedding.
So those slaves went out into the roads, and gathered all whom they found, both bad and good. And the wedding hall was filled with those who reclined at the table.
And when the king came in to see the guests, he saw there a man who did not have on a wedding garment.
So he said to him: “O sir, how did you enter here without wedding garments?” And he was silent.
Then the king said to the servants, “Bind his hands and feet, and take him away and throw him into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”
“For many claim and few are chosen.” Pay tribute to Caesar.
Then the Pharisees went and plotted how to trap him with a question.
So they sent their disciples to him, along with the Herodians, saying, “Teacher, we know that you are truthful and teach the way of God in accordance with the truth, and you do not care about anyone’s opinion, because you do not regard people with partiality.”
So tell us, what do you think? Is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?
So Jesus knew their thoughts and said to them, “Why do you test Me, you hypocrites?”
“Show me the jizya payment.” So they presented him with a dinar.
And he said to them, “To whom does this image and writing belong?”
They said to him, “To Caesar.” And he said to them, “Render therefore unto Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and unto God the things that are God’s.”
And when they heard it, they marveled and left Him, and went away. The question about the resurrection of the dead.
On that day, Sadducees came to Him, who say there is no resurrection, and they questioned Him.
Saying: “O teacher, Moses said: if a man dies without children, his brother shall marry his wife and raise up offspring for his brother.”
We had seven brothers, and the first married and died. Since he had no offspring, he left his wife to his brother.
and the second and the third to the seventh.
And finally, the woman died as well.
And on the Day of Resurrection, to whom of the seven will she be a wife? For she was a wife to all of them!"
And Jesus answered and said to them, “You are mistaken, not knowing the Scriptures nor the power of God.”
For in the resurrection they neither marry nor are given in marriage, but are like angels of God in heaven.
And as for the resurrection of the dead, have you not read what was said to you by God, who said:
“Am I not the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob? God is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living.”
And when the crowds heard it, they were astonished at His teaching. The Greatest Commandment.
Now the Pharisees, when they heard that He had silenced the Sadducees, gathered together.
And one of them, a mosquito, asked him to test it, saying:
“Teacher, which commandment is the greatest in the Law?”
And Jesus said to him, “You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind.”
This is the first and greatest commandment.
And the second is like it: love your neighbor as yourself.
“By these two commandments the whole Law and the Prophets depend.” Christ and David.
And while the Pharisees were gathered, Jesus asked them
saying, “What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is He?” They said to Him, “The son of David.”
He said to them, “How then does David by the Spirit call him Lord?” saying,
The Lord said to my Lord, “Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.”
"If David calls Him Lord, how then is His Son?"
No one could answer him with a word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him anything at all.
Warning against the scribes and Pharisees
Then Jesus addressed the crowds and his disciples.
saying, “On Moses’ seat sat the scribes and the Pharisees,
Therefore, everything they tell you to keep, keep it and do it, but do not act according to their deeds, because they say and do not act.
Indeed, they carry heavy burdens, difficult to bear, and place them on the shoulders of people, while they themselves do not want to move them even a finger.
And all their deeds they do to be seen by people: for they broaden their phylacteries and lengthen the tassels of their garments,
And they love the highest places in gatherings and the first seats in assemblies,
And greetings in the markets, and that people call them: My master, my master!
And as for you, do not call anyone ‘father’ on earth, for you have one Father, who is in heaven. And you are all brothers and sisters.
And do not call anyone on earth your father, for you have one Father, who is in heaven.
And do not call anyone ‘teacher,’ for you have one Teacher, the Messiah.
And the greatest among you will be your servant.
And whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted. Woe to scribes and Pharisees.
But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut the kingdom of heaven in people’s faces. You yourselves do not enter, and you stop those who are trying to enter.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you devour widows’ houses, and for a pretense you make long prayers. Therefore you will receive a greater condemnation.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you travel about sea and land to make one proselyte, and when you do, you make him a son of hell twice as much as yourselves.
Woe to you, blind leaders! Those who say, ‘Whoever swears by the temple, it is nothing,’ but whoever swears by the gold of the temple is bound.
O you foolish and blind! Which is greater, the gold or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
And whoever swears by the altar, it is nothing, but whoever swears by the offering that is upon it is bound [by his oath].
O foolish and blind! Which is greater, the offering or the altar that sanctifies the offering?
Indeed, whoever swears by the altar has sworn by it and by everything upon it!
And whoever swears by the Temple has sworn by it and by He who dwells within it.
And whoever swears by the heavens, he has sworn by the throne of Allah and by He who is seated upon it.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe mint and dill and cumin, and have neglected the weightier matters of the law: justice and mercy and faithfulness. These you ought to have done, and not to have left the others undone.
O blind leaders! Those who strain out a gnat and swallow a camel.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you clean the outside of the cup and the plate, while inside they are full of extortion and wickedness.
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the plate, that the outside also may be clean.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which outwardly appear beautiful, but inside are full of dead people's bones and all uncleanness.
So you also appear righteous to others, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and wickedness.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the monuments of the righteous,
And you say: “If we had been among the people of our fathers, we would not have joined them in shedding the blood of the prophets.”
And you bear witness against yourselves that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
So fill, then, the measure of your fathers.
O snakes, offspring of snakes! How will you escape the judgment of Hell?
Therefore, I send you prophets and wise men and scribes, some of whom you will kill and crucify, and some you will flog in your synagogues, and some you will chase from city to city.
So that on you may come all the righteous blood shed on earth, from the blood of Abel the righteous to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Berechiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.
Truly I tell you, all this will come upon this generation! Jesus laments Jerusalem.
O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, who kills the prophets and stones those who are sent to you! How often I have wanted to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, but you were unwilling!
Behold, your home is left to you in ruins.
For I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, ‘Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!’”.
Signs of the end times
Then Jesus left and went out of the temple, and His disciples came to Him to show Him the buildings of the temple.
And Jesus said to them, “Do you not see all these things? Truly I say to you, not one stone will be left here upon another that will not be overthrown!”
As he sat on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately, saying, “Tell us, when will this be, and what will be the sign of your coming and of the end of the age?”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “Take heed that no one deceives you.”
Many will come in my name, claiming, ‘I am the Messiah,’ and will lead many astray.
And you will hear of wars and rumors of wars. See, do not be alarmed. For all these things must come to pass, but it is not yet the end.
For nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom, and there will be famines and plagues and earthquakes in various places.
But all of this is just the beginning of the pains.
Then they will deliver you up to tribulation and kill you, and you will be hated by all nations for my name’s sake.
Then many will stumble and betray one another, and hate one another.
Many false prophets will arise and mislead many.
And due to the abundance of sin, the love of many grows cold.
But whoever perseveres to the end will be saved.
And they proclaim this good news of the kingdom throughout the whole world, as a testimony to all nations. Then the end will come.
So when you see the “abomination that causes desolation” spoken of by the prophet Daniel standing in the holy place—let the reader understand—
Then let those in Judaism flee to the mountains,
And the one on the roof should not descend to take anything from his home.
And whoever is in the field should not turn back to get his clothes.
And woe to those who are pregnant and those who are nursing in those days!
Pray that your flight does not occur in winter or on a Sabbath.
For then there will be great distress, such as has not been since the beginning of the world until now, neither shall be.
And if those days were not shortened, no flesh would be saved. But for the sake of the elect, those days will be shortened.
Then if anyone says to you, ‘Lo, here is the Christ!’ or ‘There!’ do not believe it.
For they will arise false Christs and false prophets, and they will show great signs and wonders, so as to deceive, if possible, even the elect.
I have already told you.
And if they say to you, “Behold, He is in the wilderness!” Do not go out. If they say, “Behold, He is in the secret chambers!” Do not believe them.
For just as the lightning comes from the east and shines to the west, so will the coming of the Son of Man be.
For wherever the corpse is, there the vultures gather.
And immediately after the distress of those days, the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light, and the stars will fall from the sky, and the powers of the heavens will be shaken.
Then will appear the sign of the Son of Man in heaven. And then all the tribes of the earth will mourn, and they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with great power and glory.
And He sends His angels with a great trumpet blast, and they will gather His chosen ones from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the other.
So they learned the parable from the fig tree: when its branch becomes tender and puts forth its leaves, you know that summer is near.
So you also, when you see all these things, know that it is near, right at the door.
Truly I say to you, this generation will not pass away until all these things take place.
Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away. Constant preparation.
And as for that Day and that Hour, no one knows them – not even the angels of the heavens – except My Father alone.
And as were the days of Noah, so will be the coming of the Son of Man.
For as they were in the days before the flood, they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day that Noah entered the ark,
And they did not know until the flood came and swept away all of them; so will the coming of the Son of Man be.
Then two will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two grind on the millstone, one is taken and the other left.
Be vigilant, for you do not know when your Lord will come.
And know this: if the lord of the house had known in what watch the thief would come, he would have stayed awake and would not have let his house be broken into.
Therefore, be ready yourselves, for the Son of Man will come at an hour you do not expect. Like the faithful servant.
So who is the trustworthy, wise servant whom his master appointed over his household to give them their portions of food at the proper time?
Blessed is that servant whom his master will find so doing when he returns!
Truly I say to you: He values it above all his possessions.
But if that base slave says in his heart, “My master is slow in coming.”
So he begins to strike his fellow slaves and eat and drink with the drunkards.
The master of that slave comes on a day he does not expect, and at an hour he does not know.
and he will cut him off and assign his portion with the hypocrites. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Like the ten virgins
Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins, who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
And five of them were wise, and five were foolish.
And the foolish virgins took their lamps but did not bring oil with them.
And the wise virgins took oil in their vessels with their lamps.
And while the bridegroom was delayed, they all became drowsy and fell asleep.
And at midnight a cry was heard: ‘Behold, the bridegroom comes! Go out to meet him!’
Then all those virgins rose and trimmed their lamps.
And the foolish women said to the wise women: “Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out.”
Then the wise women replied, saying: Perhaps it is not enough for us, but rather go to the merchants and buy for yourselves.
And as they went to buy, the bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the wedding, and the door was shut.
Finally, the other virgins came, saying: “Lord, Lord, open to us!”
And he answered and said, “Truly I say to you, I do not know you.”
Therefore keep watch, because you do not know on what day your Lord will come. Like the talents.
And as if a traveler called his servants and entrusted them with his possessions,
So he gave one five talents, another two, and another one, to each according to his ability. Then he went away.
So the one who received five talents went and traded with them, and made a profit of five more talents.
And so the one who took the two weights also gained two more weights.
And as for the one who was given the talent, he went and dug in the ground and hid his master’s silver.
And after a long time, the master of those slaves came and settled accounts with them.
Then came the one who had received five talents and presented five more talents, saying, “Sir, five talents you entrusted to me. See, I have earned five more talents in addition to them.”
So his master said to him, “Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will entrust you with much. Enter into the joy of your master.”
Then the one who had taken the two dinars came and said, “Sir, two dinars you entrusted to me. Here are two more that I have earned in addition to them.”
His master said to him: “Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will entrust you with much. Enter into the joy of your master.”
Then came also the one who had received one talent and said, ‘Lord, I knew you to be a hard man, reaping where you have not sown, and gathering where you have not winnowed.’
I feared and proceeded, and concealed your weight upon the earth. Behold, this is for you.
Then his master answered and said to him, ‘You wicked and lazy servant, I knew that you would harvest where you have not sown, and gather where you have not scattered seed.’
So, I should have put my silver with the money changers, for when I came, I would take what was mine with interest.
So take the full measure, and give it to him who has ten measures.
For everyone who has will be given more and will have an abundance, but from the one who does not have, even what he has will be taken away.
And cast the useless servant into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. Sheep and goats.
And when the Son of Man comes in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then he will sit on his glorious throne.
And all nations will be gathered before Him, and He will separate them from one another, as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.
Then the sheep will stand on His right hand, and the goats on the left.
Then the king will say to those on his right, “Come, you who are blessed by my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you since the foundation of the world.”
For I was hungry and you gave me food. I was thirsty and you gave me drink. I was a stranger and you welcomed me.
Naked, you clothed me. Sick, you visited me. Imprisoned, you came to me.
Then the righteous will answer him, saying, “Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you, or thirsty and give you drink?”
And when did we see you as a stranger and welcomed you, or naked and clothed you?
And when did we see you sick or in prison and come to you?
Then the king answered and said to them, “Truly I tell you, inasmuch as you did it to one of the least of these my brothers, you did it to me.”
Then He will say to those on the left, ‘Depart from Me, you cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.
Because I was hungry, you did not feed me. Because I was thirsty, you did not give me drink.
I was a stranger, and you did not welcome me. Naked, and you did not clothe me. Sick and imprisoned, and you did not visit me.
Then they will answer him, saying, ‘Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or a stranger or naked or sick or in prison, and did not serve you?’
He answered them, saying: “Truly, I say to you, as you did not do it to one of the least of these, you did not do it to me.”
So those will go to an eternal punishment, and the righteous to eternal life.
The conspiracy to kill Jesus
And when Jesus had finished all these sayings, He said to His disciples,
You know that after two days the Passover is coming, and the Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified.
Then the chief priests and the scribes and the elders of the people gathered in the house of the high priest, who was called Caiaphas,
And they plotted together to seize Jesus by deceit and kill him.
But they said, “Not during the festival, lest there be a riot among the people.” They poured perfume on Jesus.
And as Jesus was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,
A woman came up to him with an alabaster jar of very expensive perfume, and she poured it on his head while he was reclining.
And when His disciples saw it, they were indignant, saying, “Why this waste?”
"For this perfume could have been sold at a high price and given to the poor.”
Then Jesus turned, and seeing them he said, “Why do you trouble the woman? She has done a good deed for me!”
For the poor you always have with you, but you do not always have me.
For she, when she poured this perfume on My body, did so to prepare Me for burial.
Truly I say to you, wherever this gospel is preached in the whole world, what she has done will also be spoken of as a memorial of her.” Judas’ betrayal.
Then one of the twelve, named Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests.
And he said, “What will you give me to deliver him over to you?” So they weighed out for him thirty pieces of silver.
From that time he sought an opportunity to deliver him. The Passover supper with his disciples.
And on the first day of Unleavened Bread the disciples came to Jesus, saying to Him, “Where do You want us to prepare for You to eat the Passover?”
He said: “Go to the city, to so-and-so, and tell him: ‘The Teacher says: My time is near. Prepare the Passover with My disciples.’”
So the disciples did as Jesus commanded them and prepared the Passover.
And when evening came, he reclined with the Twelve.
And while they were eating, He said, “Truly I say to you, one of you will betray Me.”
So they were very saddened, and each of them began to say to Him, “Is it I, Lord?”
And he answered and said, “He who puts his hand in the dish with me is the one who will betray me!”
“The Son of Man must go, as it is written about him, but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for that man if he had not been born!”
Then Judas, betraying him, said, “Is it I, Rabbi?” He said to him, “You have said so.” The Lord’s Supper.
While they were eating, Jesus took the bread, blessed it, broke it, and gave it to the disciples, saying, “Take and eat; this is my body.”
And he took the cup and gave thanks, and gave it to them, saying, “Drink from it, all of you,
For this is my blood of the new covenant, which is shed for many for the forgiveness of sins.
And I say to you, I will no longer drink of this product of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you in my Father’s kingdom.”
Then they swam and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus foretells Peter's denial of him.
Then Jesus said to them, “All of you will be made to stumble because of Me this night, for it is written: ‘I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.’”
But after I have gone ahead of you to Galilee.
Then Peter answered and said to Him, “Even if all fall away, I will not fall away.”
Jesus said to him, “Truly I tell you, before the rooster crows, you will disown me three times.”
Peter said to him, “Even if I have to die with you, I will not disown you!” Thus all the disciples said the same. In Gethsemane.
Then Jesus came with them to a place called Gethsemane, and said to His disciples, “Sit here while I go and pray over there.”
Then he took with him Peter and the sons of Zebedee, and began to be sorrowful and deeply distressed.
Then he said to them, “My soul is sorrowful to the point of death. Remain here and stay awake with me.”
Then he advanced a little and fell face down, praying and saying, “Father, if you are willing, take this cup from me; yet not my will, but yours be done.”
Then he came to the disciples and found them sleeping, and said to Peter, “Could you not watch with me for one hour?”
Stay vigilant and pray lest you fall into trial. “The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.”
And he went away again and prayed, saying: “My Father, if it is not possible for this cup to pass from me unless I drink it, let your will be done.”
Then He came and found them sleeping again, because their eyes were heavy.
So he left them and went on, and prayed a third time, saying the exact same words.
Then he came to his disciples and said to them, “Sleep now and rest! Behold, the hour is at hand, and the Son of Man is betrayed into the hands of sinners.”
“Rise, let us go! See, my betrayer is at hand!” The arrest of Jesus.
And while he was still speaking, behold, Judas, one of the twelve, came with a great crowd with swords and clubs from the chief priests and elders of the people.
And the one who delivered him gave them a sign, saying, “The one I greet is the one. Seize him.”
Then Judas approached Jesus and said, “Greetings, Rabbi!” and kissed him.
Then Jesus said to him, “Why have you come?” Immediately, they stepped forward and laid their hands on Jesus and arrested him.
Then one of those who were with Jesus drew his sword and struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Then Jesus said to him, “Put your sword back in its place. For all who take the sword will perish by the sword!”
Do you think I cannot now ask my father to provide me with more than twelve armies of angels?
"So how do you finish the books: is it supposed to be like this?"
At that hour Jesus said to the crowds, “Have you come out with swords and clubs as against a thief? Every day I sat with you, teaching in the temple, and you did not seize me.”
“And all this happened to fulfill what was spoken by the prophets.” Then all the disciples deserted him and fled. Before the assembly of the Jews.
And those who arrested Jesus took Him to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and elders had gathered.
Now Peter was following him at a distance, into the courtyard of the high priest, and went inside and sat with the servants to see the outcome.
And the chief priests and the elders and the whole council sought false testimony against Jesus to put Him to death,
So they found none. And although many false witnesses came forward, they found none. But finally, false witnesses came forward.
And they said, “This one claims, ‘I am able to tear down God’s temple and rebuild it in three days.’”
Then the high priest asked him, “Do you have no answer? What do these men testify against you?”
Now Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest answered and said to Him, “I adjure You by the living God: Tell us whether You are the Christ, the Son of God?”
Jesus said to him, “You have said it! And I say to you, from now on you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of Power, and coming on the clouds of heaven.”
Then the high priest tore his clothes and said, “He has blasphemed! What further need do we have of witnesses? You have all heard the blasphemy!”
“What do you see?” They answered and said, “He deserves death.”
Then they spat in his face, and struck him, and some slapped him.
Saying, “Tell us, Messiah, who struck you?” Peter’s denial.
But Peter was sitting outside in the courtyard, and a servant girl came and said to him, “You also were with Jesus of Galilee!”
Then he denied before everyone, saying: “I do not know what you are saying!”
Then, as he went out into the hall, another saw him and said to those who were there, “This man also was with Jesus the Nazarene!”
And he denied again by oath: “I do not know the man!”
And after a little while, the attendant came and said to Peter, “Surely you are also one of them, for your speech betrays you!”
Then he immediately began to curse and swear: “I do not know the man!” And at that moment the rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered the word that Jesus had said to him, “Before the rooster crows, you will disown me three times.” And he went outside and wept bitterly.
Judas hangs himself.
And when it was morning, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Jesus to put him to death.
So they bound him and led him away and handed him over to Pilate, the governor, who was a Gentile.
Then, when Judas, who betrayed Him, saw that He was condemned, he regretted it and brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders.
saying: “I was wrong to shed innocent blood.” And they said: “What can we do? You are the most discerning!”
So he cast the silver into the temple and went off, then departed and hanged himself.
Then the chief priests took the silver and said, “It is not lawful to put it into the treasury, for it is the price of blood.”
Then they conferred and bought with the money the field of the potter, as a burial place for strangers.
Therefore, that field has been called “the Field of Blood” to this day.
Then was fulfilled what was spoken through the prophet Jeremiah, saying, “And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of the valued One, whom they valued among the children of Israel,”
“And they gave it to the potter’s field, as the Lord commanded me.” Before Pilate.
So Jesus stood before the governor. And the governor asked Him, saying, “Are You the King of the Jews?” And Jesus said to him, “You say.”
And while the chief priests and elders accused him, he answered nothing.
Then Pilate said to him, “Do you not hear how many they testify against you?”
He did not answer him, not even a single word, which greatly surprised the governor.
The governor used to customarily release one prisoner to the people on the holiday, whoever they desired.
And at that time they had a famous prisoner called Barabbas.
Now while they were gathered together, Pilate asked them, “Whom do you want me to release to you, Barabbas, or Jesus who is called Christ?”
because they handed him over out of envy.
And as he was sitting on the throne, his wife sent to him, saying, “Beware of that young man, for I have suffered much today in a dream because of him.”
But the chief priests and elders stirred up the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to destroy Jesus.
So the governor asked them, “Which of the two do you want me to release to you?” And they said, “Barabbas!”
Pilate asked them, “Then what shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?” They all answered, “Crucify him!”
Then the governor said, “What harm has he done?” And they cried out all the more, saying, “Crucify him!”
When Pilate saw that he could do nothing, but rather that a riot was starting, he took water and washed his hands before the crowd, saying, “I am innocent of the blood of this righteous man. See to it yourselves!”
And all the people answered and said, “His blood be on us and on our children.”
Then Pilate released Barabbas to them. And he delivered Jesus to be crucified. The soldiers mocked him.
So the governor’s soldiers took Jesus to the Praetorium and gathered the whole cohort against him.
They exposed him and dressed him in a crimson robe.
And they braided a crown of thorns and put it on His head, and a reed in His right hand. And they knelt before Him and mocked Him, saying, “Hail, King of the Jews!”
And they spat on him, and took the reed and struck him on the head.
And after they mocked him, they stripped him of his robe and put his own clothes on him, and led him away to be crucified. The crucifixion.
And as they were going out, they met a man from Cyrene, named Simon, and they compelled him to carry his cross.
And when they came to a place called Golgotha, which is said to be “the place of the skull,”
Give him a mixture of honey and bitterness to drink. And when he tasted it, he refused to drink.
And when they crucified Him, they divided His garments by casting lots, that it might be fulfilled what was spoken by the prophet: “They divided My garments among them, and for My clothing they cast lots.”
Then they sat to guard him there.
And they put above His head the accusation written against Him: “This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.”
Then there were crucified with Him two thieves, one on His right and one on His left.
And those who passed by were rowing upon it, shaking their heads.
saying: “You who destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days, save yourself! If you are the Son of God, come down from the cross!”
And so also the chief priests, with the scribes and elders, mocked Him, saying:
“He saves others, but cannot save himself! If he is the King of Israel, let him come down now from the cross, and we will believe him!”
“If He is God, let Him rescue Him now, if He desires! For He said, ‘I am the Son of God!’”
And so also those two robbers who were crucified with Him were reviling Him. Death.
And from the sixth hour there was darkness over all the earth until the ninth hour.
And about the ninth hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, “Eli, Eli, lema sabachthani?” that is, “My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?”
Then some of those standing there, when they heard it, said, “It is Elijah who is calling.”
And one of them ran for a moment and took a sponge and filled it with vinegar and put it on a reed and gave it to him to drink.
And the rest said, “Let him be, let us see whether Elijah will come to save him!”
And Jesus cried out again with a loud voice, and breathed His last.
And the veil of the temple was torn in two, from top to bottom. The earth quaked, and the rocks were split.
And the graves were opened, and many bodies of the saints who had fallen asleep rose again.
And they came out of the tombs after His resurrection, and entered the holy city and appeared to many.
Now the centurion and those who were with him, guarding Jesus, when they saw the earthquake and the things that happened, feared greatly and said, “Truly this was the Son of God!”
There were also many women looking on from afar, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering to Him.
And among them were Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee. The Burial.
And when evening came, a rich man from Arimathea named Joseph, who was also a disciple of Jesus, came.
Then he advanced to Pilate and requested the body of Jesus. Pilate therefore ordered that the body be given.
Then Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth,
And he laid him in his new tomb which he had hewn out of the rock, and rolled a large stone to the door of the tomb and departed.
And Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb.
And on the day following the preparation, the chief priests and Pharisees gathered together to Pilate.
saying: “Lord, you have remembered that the deceiver, while still alive, said, ‘I will rise again after three days.’
“Therefore, with strict security, guard the tomb until the third day, lest His disciples come by night and steal Him, and say to the people, ‘He has risen from the dead!’—and the last deception will be worse than the first.”
Then Pilate said to them, “You have a guard. Go, secure him as you know how.”
So they departed and secured the tomb with guards and sealed the stone.
The Resurrection
And after the Sabbath, at dawn on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary came to see the tomb.
And when a great earthquake occurs, [it will be] because the angels of the Lord descend from the sky and come and roll the stone from the gate and sit upon it.
And his appearance was like lightning, and his garments were white as snow.
And from His fear, the guards trembled and became like the dead.
And the angel answered and said to the women, “Do not be afraid, for I know that you seek Jesus who was crucified.”
He is not here, for He has risen as He said! Come, see the place where the Lord lay.
“Go quickly and tell his disciples, ‘He has risen from the dead, and he is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him. Behold, I have told you.’”
So they departed quickly from the tomb with fear and great joy, running to tell His disciples.
And as they were walking along, they reported to their disciples that they had met Jesus and he had said to them, “Peace be with you.” Then they came forward and grasped his feet and worshiped him.
Then Jesus said to them, “Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, where they will see me.” The guards’ report.
And as they were going, a group of guards came to the city and told the chief priests everything that had happened.
So they gathered with the elders, consulted, and gave the army a great deal of silver.
Saying: “Say that his disciples came by night and stole him while we were sleeping.”
And if he hears this from the governor, we will appeal to him and reassure you.”
So they took the silver and did as they were instructed, and this story spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus Appears to His Disciples.
Now the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain to which Jesus had directed them.
And when they saw it, they prostrated to it, but some of them doubted.
Then Jesus came up and spoke to them, saying: “All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.”
Therefore go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
And teach them to observe all that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you always, to the end of the age.” Amen.
John the Baptist prepares the way.
The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God,
As it is written in the prophets: “Behold, I send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way before you.”
A voice crying in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.
And John was baptizing in the wilderness and proclaiming a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
And all Judea and the people of Jerusalem went out to him and were baptized by him in the Jordan River, confessing their sins.
And John was clothed in camel’s hair, with a leather belt around his waist, and he ate locusts and wild honey.
And he was preaching, saying: “After me comes one who is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to stoop down and untie.”
“I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit.” The baptism of Jesus Christ and his temptation.
And in those days Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan.
And as He was coming up out of the water, He saw the heavens opening, and the Spirit descending like a dove upon Him.
And a voice from the heavens said, “You are my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.”
And at that time, the Spirit led Him into the wilderness.
And he was in the wilderness forty days, being tempted by the devil. And he was with the wild beasts, and the angels ministered to him. Calling of the first disciples.
And after John had surrendered, Jesus came to Galilee, proclaiming the good news of the kingdom of God.
And He said, “The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent and believe in the gospel.”
And as He walked by the Sea of Galilee, He saw Simon and Andrew his brother casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
Then Jesus said to them, “Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.”
And for a while, they left their nets and followed Him.
Then He passed on from there and saw James the son of Zebedee and John his brother, who were in the boat mending their nets.
So he called them for a time. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired servants and followed him. An unclean spirit was cast out.
Then they entered Capernaum, and immediately on the Sabbath He entered the synagogue and began to teach.
So they were astonished by his teaching, for he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
And in their synagogue there was a man with an unclean spirit, and he cried out
saying: “Ah! What have we to do with you, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are—the Holy One of God!”
Then Jesus rebuked him, saying, “Be silent, and come out of him!”
And the unclean spirit threw him to the ground and cried out with a loud voice, and came out of him.
And they were all amazed, so much that some questioned each other, saying, “What is this? What is this new teaching? For He commands even the unclean spirits with authority, and they obey Him!”
And his story spread at that time throughout the entire region surrounding Galilee. The healing of Hama and Simeon and others.
And when they departed from the synagogue, they came into the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John.
And Simon’s mother-in-law lay sick with a fever, and they told him about her immediately.
So he advanced and established it, holding it by the hand, and the fever left her immediately, and she began to serve them.
And when evening came, as the sun was setting, they brought to him all those who were sick or possessed by demons.
And the whole city was gathered at the gate.
And he healed many who were sick with various diseases, and cast out many demons. And he would not let the demons speak, because they knew who he was. Proclamation in Galilee.
And in the early morning, very early, he got up and went out and went to a secluded place, and he used to pray there.
And Simon and those with him followed him.
And when they found him, they said to him, “Everyone is looking for you.”
And He said to them, “Let us go to the neighboring villages so that I may preach there also, for that is why I came forth.”
So he was preaching in their synagogues throughout all Galilee and casting out demons. Healing a leper.
Then a leper came to him, seeking him, kneeling and saying to him, “If you will, you can make me clean.”
And Jesus, moved with compassion, stretched out His hand and touched him, and said to him, “I will; be cleansed!”
And as he was speaking, the leprosy left him and he was cleansed.
So reprimand him and send him forth for the appointed time.
And He said to him, “See, do not tell anyone anything, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer for your purification what Moses commanded, as a testimony to them.”
And as for him, he went out and began to proclaim it loudly and spread the news, until he could no longer enter a city openly, but was outside in deserted places, and they were coming to him from every direction.
Healing of a paralyzed person.
Then He entered Capernaum after some days, and it was heard that He was in the house.
And so a large crowd gathered that there was no longer room, not even around the door. And He was speaking to them in parables.
And they came to Him, bringing a paralyzed man carried by four.
And when they could not find a way to get to Him through the crowd, they went up to the roof and removed the tiles above where He was. And when they had opened it, they let down the bed on which the paralytic was lying.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralytic, “Son, your sins are forgiven.”
And some of the scribes were sitting there, reasoning in their hearts:
Why does he speak like this with insults? Who can forgive sins except Allah alone?
But Jesus, perceiving in His spirit that they were thinking to themselves, said to them, “Why are you thinking this in your hearts?”
Which is easier, to say to the paralyzed: your sins are forgiven, or to say: get up, take your mat, and walk?
But that you may know that the Son of Man has power on earth to forgive sins”—He said to the paralytic:
And I say to you, rise and carry your bed and go to your home!
So he rose with the time and carried the bed and went out before all, until everyone was astonished and they glorified God, saying: “We have never seen anything like this!” A Levite’s call.
Then He went out again by the sea, and all the crowd came to Him and He taught them.
And as he passed by, he saw Levi the son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax booth, and he said to him, “Follow me.” And he rose and followed him.
And it happened, as He reclined in the house, that many tax collectors and sinners were reclining with Jesus and His disciples, for there were many, and they were following Him.
And the scribes and the Pharisees, when they saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, said to his disciples, “Why does he eat and drink with tax collectors and sinners?”
When Jesus heard this, He said to them, “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.” The question about fasting.
And the disciples of John and of the Pharisees were fasting. Then they came and said to Him, “Why do the disciples of John and of the Pharisees fast, but Your disciples do not fast?”
And Jesus said to them, “Can the guests at a wedding fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.”
But days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they will fast in those days.
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth. For the patch taken from the new makes a tear, and the new patch will not match the old, so it will become worse.
And no one puts new wine into old wineskins, lest the new wine burst the wineskins, and the wine is spilled and the wineskins are ruined. But they put new wine into fresh wineskins.” Lord of the Sabbath.
And He was passing through the grainfields on the Sabbath, and His disciples began to pluck the heads of grain as they went.
Then the Pharisees said to Him, “Behold, why do they do what is not lawful on the Sabbath?”
And He said to them, “Have you not ever read what David did when he was in need and he and those with him were hungry?”
“How did he enter the house of God in the days of Abijath the high priest and eat the bread of the Presence, which is not to be eaten by anyone but the priests, and give some to those who were with him as well?”
Then He said to them, “The Sabbath was made for mankind, and not mankind for the Sabbath.”
“If the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath as well.”
Healing on Saturday
Then He entered the synagogue again, and there was a man there with a withered hand.
So they began to watch Him, to see if He would heal on the Sabbath, so that they might accuse Him.
Then He said to the man with the withered hand, “Come forward into the middle!”
Then he said to them, “Is it lawful to do good on the Sabbath, or to do evil, to save life or to kill?” And they remained silent.
So he looked around at them with anger, saddened by the hardness of their hearts, and said to the man, “Extend your hand.” And he extended it, and his hand was restored, healthy like the other.
So the Pharisees went immediately and conferred with the Herodians on how to put him to death. The crowds followed Jesus.
Then Jesus went with his disciples to the sea, and a great crowd from Galilee and Judea followed him.
And from Jerusalem, and from Idumea, and from beyond the Jordan. And a great multitude from Tyre and Sidon came, hearing how many things He had done.
So he told his disciples to accompany him in a small boat for the sake of the crowd, so that they would not press on him.
Because he had healed many, so many with diseases were pressing on him to touch him.
And the unclean spirits, when they saw Him, fell down before Him and cried out, saying, “You are the Son of God!”
And he instructed them repeatedly not to reveal it. The selection of the Twelve Apostles.
Then he went up the mountain and called to those he wanted, and they came to him.
And he appointed twelve to be with him, to send them out to preach,
And they will have authority to heal the sick and cast out demons.
And He named Simon Peter.
And James the son of Zebedee and John the brother of James, and He named them Boanerges, which means “sons of thunder.”
and Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus, and Simon the Zealot,
And Judas Iscariot, who betrayed Him. Then they came to a house. Jesus and Beelzebul.
So they gathered in such large numbers that they could not even eat bread.
And when his relatives heard, they went out to seize him, for they said, “He is out of his mind!”
Now the scribes who came down from Jerusalem said, “He has Beelzebul, and by the ruler of the demons He casts out demons.”
Then he called to them and said to them in parables: “How can a demon cast out a demon?”
And if a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand.
And if a house is divided against itself, that house cannot stand.
C’est une question complexe, mais en résumé, l’intelligence artificielle générative a le potentiel de révolutionner de nombreux secteurs, de la création de contenu à la découverte de médicaments, en passant par l’automatisation des tâches répétitives. Cependant, il est crucial de prendre en compte les défis éthiques et sociétaux qu’elle soulève, notamment en matière de biais, de désinformation et d’impact sur l’emploi. Une approche responsable et encadrée est donc essentielle pour maximiser les bénéfices de cette technologie tout en minimisant ses risques.